    "K_t": "Evidence:\n[1] Literary critic Roberto González Echevarría calls the work \"the most important book written by a Latin American in any discipline or genre\".\n[2] Along with Domingo Faustino Sarmiento's Facundo, \"Amalia\" can be seen as an early precursor to the Latin American dictator novel through its strong criticism of caudillo Juan Manuel de Rosas, who ruled Argentina with a strong fist from 1829 to 1852.\n[3] Domingo Faustino Sarmiento (February 15, 1811 – September 11, 1888) was an Argentine activist, intellectual, writer, statesman and the seventh President of Argentina.\n[4] It is a cornerstone of Latin American literature: a work of creative non-fiction that helped to define the parameters for thinking about the region's development, modernization, power, and culture.\n\nNotes:\n[bridge] Domingo Faustino Sarmiento died in 1888."
